   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.1. Increased capacity of Member States to create an enabling environment for entrepreneurship and sustainable enterprises</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Cooperative Work Agency (CWA) has taken the lead in developing the Cooperative Sectoral Strategy for 2024-2029, aligning it with the guidelines set by the Prime Minister&apos;s Office in &quot;The Guide to Prepare Strategies for Ministries and Government Agencies.&quot; The strategy&apos;s overarching goal is to strengthen capabilities, enhance competencies, and improve the overall performance of the cooperative sector. 

The vision for the new strategy was developed through a consultative process that engaged social partners and stakeholders. This inclusive approach allowed for a review of the previous strategy&apos;s progress, lessons learned, and achievements. The valuable insights gathered from this review were then utilized in formulating the new strategy, ensuring it is forward-looking and effective in achieving its intended objectives.

 Furthermore, the CWA has successfully completed the capacity needs assessment This assessment has identified the capacity needs for the Cooperative Development Institute (CDI) and Fund (CDF) establishment and for training and financial services required for cooperatives under these institutions. As a result, the groundwork has been laid for both CDF and CDI  to  effectively fulfil their regulatory and promotional functions. This development will create a conducive ecosystem for cooperatives to thrive independently, fostering their growth and success in the future.
 The ILO has provided the CWA with policy support, consultations and technical inputs towards the review of the previous cooperative sector strategy and formulation of the new one. 
The ILO supported CWA in organising a consultative workshop with 50 members from CWA and other relevant institutions including government entities, local institutions and cooperative sectorial unions.

The ILO  also provided support to obtain the necessary technical experts to conduct the capacity needs assessment undertaken by CWA.

Moreover, the ILO provided the CWA with intensive training programs on &quot;Think.COOP&quot; and &quot;Start.COOP.&quot; Additionally, the CWA&apos;s cooperative auditors received specialized training in Cooperative Management and Cooperative Audit. These capacity development efforts have transformed the CWA&apos;s capabilities, resulting in improved service delivery and an empowered workforce ready to drive productive cooperative development in the OPT. Moreover, seventeen staff members, including eight women, underwent training in Advanced Strategic Planning, leading to six active cooperatives devising strategic plans for enhanced productivity. Furthermore, ten participants, including six core CWA staff, received training in the Statistical Package for Social Sciences (SPSS), enhancing their statistical analysis capabilities.</narrative>
   </description>
