   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.2. Strengthened capacity of enterprises and their support systems to enhance productivity and sustainability</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government, the social partners and agricultural value chain actors made significant progress towards developing and operationalizing programmes to promote entrepreneurship, and business resilience including through inclusive models and cooperatives. 
a)	An action plan was introduced to develop value chains in key agricultural sectors, with a focus on employment promotion. The Dairy and Honey sub-sectors in the Jordan Valley were identified as having potential for inclusive growth and decent employment opportunities, especially for women and youth. The plan involves collaboration with tripartite partners, and sector stakeholders to implement measures that improve production practices, create safe working environments, and increase profitability in these sectors.
b)	Local development service providers in Gaza adopted ILO business training packages, as 25 business development specialists, half of whom are women, have become certified Start and Improve Your Business- SIYB trainers.
c)	The General Cooperatives Union has taken the lead in the design of feasibility and business plans for its sectorial unions (Agriculture, Housing, Savings &amp; Credit, Consumer, and Handicraft) with the aim of enhancing the service delivery of these unions to their members. As part of the overall strategy, a resources mobilization plan was prepared aiming to facilitate partnerships with multiple stakeholders. 
d)	As a result of the collaborative efforts of the Palestinian General Federation of Trade Unions and the Cooperative Work Agency, new and innovative forms of cooperatives were promoted with the establishment of Souq Al Manshiyeh, that leverage women and youth&apos;s technological expertise to compete with private sector entities in the Palestinian market. PGFTU and CWA have worked closely with other stakeholders to establish and register the cooperative, to empower women by enhancing their skills and organizing their efforts, ultimately improving product quality.
 The ILO contributed to the results achieved by the Government, agricultural value chain actors and the General Cooperatives entities through the following:
a)	The ILO facilitated the consultative process to identify sectors to be targeted by the Value Chain Analysis and to validate the results of the study. Afterwards, the ILO supported the implementation of key initiatives addressing constraints to value chain development through provision of technical expertise and support. 
b)	The ILO provided a 12-day Training of Trainers (ToT) workshop to 25 business development specialists, half of whom are women, have become certified Start and Improve Your Business- SIYB trainers. To complete their certification, the trainers followed to deliver the training to 320 beneficiaries of the ILO project, &#x201c;Enhanced Resilience of MSMEs and Improved Access to Decent Jobs in Gaza&#x2019;&#x2019;. 
c)	Based on a study of sectoral cooperative unions and the General Cooperative Union, the ILO developed an action plan to strengthen their governance structures and services provision for primary cooperatives. The ILO has assisted sectorial unions to prepare feasibility studies and business plans, and on resource mobilization and capacity development through provision of experts to guide, and capacity building activities.
d)	The ILO has supported the PGFTU in capacity building activities for the Souq Al Manshiyeh cooperative as well as produced promotional material for them.</narrative>
   </description>
